And live by those.

Here's my thought on the script, though:

Basically, what I'm probably thinking of is less like a module, and more like
a packaging system. Why? Because I want to call the module in order to register
modules, same for unregistering. And in order to find out where the config files
are there's a standard search path. For example, for the text commands they
feature three or four main components (depending on how you scope): The function
bodies, the auxilary functions and data, permissions, and the help commnd. We
may count the fifth as the command text itself, which I've often used as a key.

There's more basic ideas like I've noted elsehwere (using bitmasks for
permissions), but that's more about data types. Either way. The main thing the
module would have callbacks. A single event arrival wouldn't necessarily even
be featured in the script that uses this. This would also give more flexibility
with ordering execution of features.

This could mirror the Lua State and Thread stack in that each arrival itself has
a corresponding stack. Either we run those through a loop or we compose them.
Or maybe more simply we could make it so that each command that registers lists
which events it hooks, whether it blocks, and whether it mutates the data.

The mutating the data part has some complexity to it because clearly you can't
make the same assumptions after its been mutated, but you can't necessarily rule
it out either. So, perhaps there's a warning that goes with it.
